ithin a couple of years they  may be right this time that their  forecasts and I think they should be  very cautious about taking more action  because they have lurched from a  monetary policy that was flagrantly too  permissive and very inflationary to one  which is now very tight and I think it's  quite tight enough to do the job we need  to do which is to get inflation done  well then the bank of England is  independent as we know and they feel  that it's necessary to stabilize the  pound by increasing interest rates  significantly  well it's not their role or remit  stabilize the pound and they won't be  able to stabilize the pound uh the pound  is buffeted by huge forces in in Global  Currency markets where uh collectively  the the World Trade has trade far more  money than the British government could  throw at it the last time we had a  policy of trying to defend a particular  level of the pound it was particularly  disastrous policy it was called European  exchange rate mechanism and it first of  all gave us an inflation and then it  gave us a recession we don't want boom  bust and trying to Target the currency  would be a supreme error which would do  damage to our prosperity  not as disastrous for the Pounders we're  seeing at the moment some might suggest  do you think the bank of England is  overreacting  well they haven't done anything yet and  I'm very pleased they haven't I fully  supported potentially an increase in in  interest rates to six percent that's  what is being speculated do you think  that would be an overreaction yeah well  that isn't a statement to the bank of  England I'm pleased to say the bank of  England has wisely decided it doesn't  need an emergency meeting or an  emergency interest rate rise and why  would they because as I said their  forecasts are that inflation will come  back down to Target within a couple of  years and that they have already put up  interest rates on several occasions in  the past more importantly they have 